\title{Dense Final-Step Inputs: Expressivity Invariance, Boundary\\ Conditioning, and the State Horizon of Recurrent Carry Chains}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A recurrent answer path trained on five-digit least-significant-digit-first base-$10$ addition attains perfect accuracy at the training length and collapses at length eight, even though the underlying transition rule is depth-uniform. We isolate the cause by a controlled decomposition and prove three things. \emph{(i) Transition half.} The carry automaton satisfies the exact depth-uniform invariant $\val(d,n)+c_n b^n=\val(a,n)+\val(b,n)$ for all $n$, and any learned step function pointwise correct on the finitely many reachable triples $(x,y,c)$ with $x,y<b$, $c\le 1$ --- exactly the triples any depth-$\ge 2$ training set exercises --- is automatically correct at every depth. The length wall is therefore not an expressivity obstruction. \emph{(ii) Boundary half.} The end-of-sequence (EOS) input acts on the cell only through $v=We\in\R^h$; for every width $d\ge 1$ the realisable set of such $v$ is all of $\R^h$, so EOS width adds no representable function. Yet under gradient flow on the factorised parameterisation the induced velocity is $\dot v=-(\|e\|^2 I+WW^\T)g$, a preconditioned descent whose gain $\langle g,-\dot v\rangle=\|e\|^2\|g\|^2+\|W^\T g\|^2$ is at least $dc^2\|g\|^2$ for per-coordinate initialisation scale $c$ --- linear in the EOS width. Hence the quadratic boundary loss contracts as $e^{-2dc^2t}$ and a sufficient training budget scales like $1/d$. \emph{(iii) Horizon half.} For a contractive cell ($\|Az\|\le\lambda\|z\|$, $\lambda<1$) and any bounded linear readout there is a finite depth beyond which every fixed decision margin is lost; a final-step gain $m\ge1$ extends usable depth by exactly $k\ge\log m/\log(1/\lambda)$ steps. These results explain a decisive control in which two arms with byte-identical cell and head weights, differing only in EOS width ($384$ versus $20$), score $1.0000$ versus $0.0259$ at length eight. The synthesis --- the \emph{dense-final-step law} --- is that boundary-input width is invisible to the function class and visible to the optimiser, and that boundary richness buys usable depth only logarithmically.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} carry chain, length generalisation, boundary conditioning, factorised gradient flow, preconditioning, contraction, state horizon, recurrent readout.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The phenomenon}
Least-significant-digit-first addition is the canonical stress test for length generalisation. The task has a two-line exact solution with a one-bit state, the training distribution exercises every reachable state transition, and yet models trained at length $n=5$ routinely fail at $n=8$. In the round of experiments analysed here, a recurrent carry cell over a trained encoder's per-column features reached full-sequence accuracy $1.0000$ at $n=5,6,7,8$, while a bare recurrent cell over raw one-hot digit inputs reached $1.0000$ at $n=5$ and $0.002$--$0.08$ at $n=8$ (chance $10^{-9}$).

\subsection{Three hypotheses and their fate}
Three mutually exclusive hypotheses were tested, each isolating one architectural variable:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{H1 (capacity).} A capacity-matched raw recurrent cell at $471{,}582$ parameters (versus $335{,}242$ for the cure) scored $0.0078$, $0.0063$ at $n=8$: \emph{refuted}.
\item \textbf{H2 (representation).} A fixed \emph{untrained} random $384$-dimensional projection of the same one-hots scored $1.0000$, $1.0000$: the strong form is \emph{refuted}, only a weak dimensional form survives.
\item \textbf{H3 (position).} Raw one-hots plus an $8$-dimensional sinusoidal position schedule ($28$-dimensional inputs) scored $0.0049$, $0.0049$: \emph{refuted}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The decisive control}
Pad the raw $20$-dimensional one-hot digit input with $364$ permanently zero coordinates: the digit pathway carries exactly the information it carried before. But the EOS token of this architecture is a \emph{dense learned vector of the full input width}, presented at the final carry step; for the padded arm it is $384$-dimensional and the ``dead'' columns are alive for it. Because parameters are drawn in the same construction order, the recurrent cell and output head of the padded arm and of a variant with a $20$-dimensional EOS are \emph{byte-identical for a fixed seed}; the arms differ in exactly one architectural variable.

