Protocol: plain $n=5$ LSB-first base-$10$ $a+b=c$, batch size $256$, $12{,}000$ AdamW steps, teacher-forced evaluation at $n=5,6,7,8$ on $2048$ fresh draws per length. Two features drive everything below. The final-carry probe column is \emph{flat}: the recurrent transition retains its accuracy in every arm, so the failure is a \emph{readout} failure at the boundary. And the raw baseline is a \emph{distribution} ($0.0020$ to $0.6997$ over seven seeds), not a hard wall; an earlier two-seed reading undersampled it, though the qualitative conclusion ($0/7$ reach $1.0$) stands.

\section{Notation}
$b\ge 1$ is the base; digit streams are functions $\N\to\N$ and $\val(f,n)=\sum_{i<n}f_ib^i$. The cell has hidden width $h$; the EOS vector is $e\in\R^d$ and $W\in\R^{h\times d}$ is the input matrix restricted to the EOS columns. Norms are Euclidean.

\section{The transition half: the carry chain is exactly length-general}

\begin{definition}[carry automaton]
For streams $a,b$ and base $\beta$ set $c_0=0$ and
\[c_{i+1}=\left\lfloor\frac{a_i+b_i+c_i}{\beta}\right\rfloor,\qquad d_i=(a_i+b_i+c_i)\bmod\beta.\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[one-bit state]
If $\beta>0$ and $a_i,b_i<\beta$ for all $i$, then $c_n\le 1$ for all $n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: if $c_n\le1$ then $a_n+b_n+c_n\le 2\beta-1$, so $c_{n+1}\le\lfloor(2\beta-1)/\beta\rfloor<2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[exact depth-uniform invariant]
For every base $\beta$, all streams $a,b$, and every $n\ge 0$,
\[\val(d,n)+c_n\beta^{\,n}=\val(a,n)+\val(b,n).\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. Division with remainder gives $d_n+\beta c_{n+1}=a_n+b_n+c_n$; multiplying by $\beta^n$, expanding $\val(\cdot,n+1)=\val(\cdot,n)+(\cdot)_n\beta^n$ and $\beta^{n+1}=\beta^n\beta$, the claim reduces by ring rearrangement to the inductive hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[model recurrence, reachability, local correctness]
For $T:\N^3\to\N^2$ set $\hat c_0=0$, $\hat c_{i+1}=T(a_i,b_i,\hat c_i)_2$, $\hat d_i=T(a_i,b_i,\hat c_i)_1$. A triple $(x,y,c)$ is \emph{reachable} if $x<\beta$, $y<\beta$, $c\le 1$; $T$ is \emph{locally correct} if $T(x,y,c)=\big((x+y+c)\bmod\beta,\ \lfloor(x+y+c)/\beta\rfloor\big)$ on every reachable triple. For $\beta=10$ there are $200$ such triples, all exercised by depth-$\ge 2$ training data.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[local-to-global transfer]
Let $\beta>0$, let $T$ be locally correct, and let $a_i,b_i<\beta$ for all $i$. Then $\hat c_n=c_n$ and $\hat d_n=d_n$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction. The true state lies in $\{0,1\}$, and by the inductive hypothesis the model's state equals it, so the triple presented at step $n$ is reachable and local correctness applies verbatim. The system never leaves the finite verified region.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[no expressivity wall]
Under those hypotheses $\val(\hat d,n)+\hat c_n\beta^{\,n}=\val(a,n)+\val(b,n)$ for every $n$; and such a $T$ exists (the true transition), so the hypothesis is not vacuous.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[sharpness]
If $T(x,y,0)_1\neq(x+y)\bmod\beta$ for one reachable triple, then on the constant streams $a\equiv x$, $b\equiv y$ the model already emits a wrong digit at step $0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
The length wall cannot be blamed on the representational power of the recurrent cell: a correct, exactly depth-uniform step table exists, is finite, is pinned down by the training distribution, and generalises to arbitrary depth. This matches the flat final-carry probe column.
\end{remark}

\section{The boundary half: EOS width is invisible to the class, visible to the optimiser}

\subsection{Expressivity invariance}

\begin{definition}[effective boundary bias]
For $W\in\R^{h\times d}$ and $e\in\R^d$, the \emph{boundary bias} is $v=We\in\R^h$: the entire contribution of the learned EOS vector to the cell's pre-activation at the final carry step.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[surjectivity]
For every $d\ge1$ and every $v\in\R^h$ there exist $W$ and $e$ with $We=v$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $e$ the first standard basis vector and every column of $W$ equal to $v$; then $(We)_i=v_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[no expressivity gain from EOS width]
For any $d_1,d_2\ge1$,
\[\{We: W\in\R^{h\times d_1},e\in\R^{d_1}\}=\R^h=\{We: W\in\R^{h\times d_2},e\in\R^{d_2}\}.\]
In particular the $20$- and $384$-dimensional EOS of the identical-weights control span exactly the same class of boundary contributions.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Gradient flow on the factorised parameterisation}
Let the loss depend on the parameters only through $v=We$ and write $g=\nabla_v L$. The chain rule gives $\nabla_W L=ge^\T$, $\nabla_e L=W^\T g$, so gradient flow is $\dot W=-ge^\T$, $\dot e=-W^\T g$ and $\dot v=\dot We+W\dot e$.

\begin{definition}
$\drift(W,e,g):=(-ge^\T)e+W(-W^\T g)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[outer-product identity]
$(ge^\T)e=\|e\|^2g$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[closed form of the boundary drift]
$\dot v=\drift(W,e,g)=-\big(\|e\|^2I+WW^\T\big)g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first term is the outer-product identity with a sign; the second is $W(W^\T g)=(WW^\T)g$, a Fubini exchange of summations.
\end{proof}

The matrix $\|e\|^2I+WW^\T$ is symmetric positive semidefinite: the factorised parameterisation performs \emph{preconditioned} descent on $v$.

\subsection{The boundary gain and its dependence on the width}

\begin{theorem}[exact descent rate]
$\langle g,-\dot v\rangle=\|e\|^2\|g\|^2+\|W^\T g\|^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
$\langle g,-\dot v\rangle\ge\|e\|^2\|g\|^2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[sharpness]
For $W=0$ the bound is an equality, so the EOS norm is the whole of the guaranteed gain; the $WW^\T$ term can only help.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[gain is linear in EOS width]
If $|e_j|\ge c\ge0$ for all $j$, then $\|e\|^2\ge dc^2$ and hence $\langle g,-\dot v\rangle\ge d\,c^2\,\|g\|^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[strict monotonicity]
For $d_1<d_2$, fixed $c>0$ and $g\neq0$: $d_1c^2\|g\|^2<d_2c^2\|g\|^2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[boundary dichotomy]
Let $1\le d_1<d_2$, $c>0$, $g\neq0$. Then simultaneously the realisable boundary contributions at widths $d_1$ and $d_2$ are \emph{the same set}, and the guaranteed gradient-flow gain at width $d_2$ is \emph{strictly larger}. Any measured difference between two such arms is therefore an optimisation/conditioning effect, never a capacity one.
\end{theorem}

With $c$ held fixed, $d:20\to384$ multiplies the guaranteed gain by $19.2$.

\subsection{Contraction and the training-budget law}

\begin{definition}
$\mathcal{L}(v):=\tfrac12\|v-v^\star\|^2$ for a target boundary bias $v^\star$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Gr\"onwall contraction]
Let $v(t)$ be differentiable with velocity $w(t)$ and suppose $\kappa\|v(t)-v^\star\|^2\le\langle v(t)-v^\star,-w(t)\rangle$ for all $t$. Then $\mathcal{L}(v(t))\le\mathcal{L}(v(0))e^{-2\kappa t}$ for $t\ge0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$L'(t)=\langle v(t)-v^\star,w(t)\rangle\le-2\kappa L(t)$, so $F(t)=L(t)e^{2\kappa t}$ has $F'\le0$ and is antitone.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[dense-EOS exponential decay]
If $v$ evolves by the factorised gradient flow with residual gradient $g(t)=v(t)-v^\star$ and the EOS coordinates never fall below scale $c$, then $\mathcal{L}(v(t))\le\mathcal{L}(v(0))e^{-2dc^2t}$: the contraction exponent is linear in the EOS width.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[sufficient budget]
With rate $2\kappa>0$, any $t\ge\log(\mathcal{L}_0/\varepsilon)/(2\kappa)$ gives $\mathcal{L}_0e^{-2\kappa t}\le\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[$1/d$ budget law]
At $\kappa=dc^2$ the sufficient budget $\log(\mathcal{L}_0/\varepsilon)/(2dc^2)$ is strictly decreasing in $d$: for $d_1<d_2$, $c>0$ and $0<\varepsilon<\mathcal{L}_0$,
\[\frac{\log(\mathcal{L}_0/\varepsilon)}{2d_2c^2}<\frac{\log(\mathcal{L}_0/\varepsilon)}{2d_1c^2}.\]
\end{corollary}

\section{The horizon half: contraction, readout margins, and what boundary gain buys}

\begin{definition}[affine cell]
On a normed space $E$ one step is $f(x)=Ax+u$; note $f(x)-f(y)=A(x-y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[geometric collapse of state separation]
If $\|Az\|\le\lambda\|z\|$ with $\lambda\ge0$, then $\|f^{[n]}(x)-f^{[n]}(y)\|\le\lambda^n\|x-y\|$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[readout margin collapse]
Let $r$ be linear with $\|r(z)\|\le R\|z\|$, $R\ge0$, and $\|x-y\|\le\Delta$. If $\lambda^n\Delta R<\gamma$ then $|r(f^{[n]}(x))-r(f^{[n]}(y))|<\gamma$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[the state horizon exists]
Let $0\le\lambda<1$, $R,\Delta\ge0$, $\gamma>0$. There is a finite $N$ with: for all $n\ge N$ and all $x,y$ with $\|x-y\|\le\Delta$, $|r(f^{[n]}(x))-r(f^{[n]}(y))|<\gamma$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\lambda=0$ take $N=1$; if $\Delta R=0$ take $N=0$; otherwise choose $N$ with $\lambda^N<\gamma/(\Delta R)$ and use monotonicity of $\lambda^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is a genuine impossibility statement: no training procedure and no parameter count evades it; only a less contractive cell or additional boundary gain can extend the horizon. Since $\lambda,\Delta,R,\gamma$ vary with initialisation, different seeds have different horizons --- hence a \emph{distribution} of length-$8$ accuracies ($0.0020$ to $0.6997$) rather than a threshold. It also explains the flat carry probe: a one-bit state needs only a coarse margin, whereas a ten-way digit readout needs a fine one.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[boundary gain buys depth]
Let $\lambda\ge0$, $DR\ge0$ and $\lambda^N\cdot DR<\gamma$. If $m\lambda^k\le1$ then $\lambda^{N+k}(m\cdot DR)<\gamma$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[logarithmic depth law]
For $0<\lambda<1$ and $m\ge1$, $m\lambda^k\le1$ holds as soon as $k\ge\log m/\log(1/\lambda)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Multiplying the boundary gain by a constant extends the usable unroll depth by the constant additive amount $\log m/\log(1/\lambda)$: depth enters the margin exponentially while gain enters multiplicatively. Combined with the $d$-linear gain, usable depth should grow like $a+b\log d$ with $b=1/\log(1/\lambda)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The dense-final-step law}
Assembling the three halves: in a state-augmented sequential answer path,
\begin{enumerate}
\item the recurrent \emph{transition} is not the bottleneck --- an exactly depth-uniform solution exists and is forced by pointwise correctness on the finitely many reachable state--input triples;
\item the width of the \emph{final-step (EOS) input} adds no representable boundary function whatsoever;
\item but it strictly increases the guaranteed gradient-flow gain on the effective boundary bias, linearly in the width, with a $1/d$ sufficient training budget;
\item and, because a contractive cell has a provable state horizon for every bounded readout, that gain converts into usable depth only logarithmically.
\end{enumerate}

Corrections to earlier readings: the cure is not the encoder's content-rich features (an untrained random projection cures $5/5$; raw one-hots padded with $364$ dead dimensions cure $4/4$); the digit pathway may be raw, the failing configuration's problem being its $20$-dimensional EOS; the raw state horizon is real but heavy-tailed in seed variance; and EOS richness must exceed the digit count by a large factor, a $28$-dimensional learned EOS still failing at $0.0049$, so the threshold lies strictly in $(28,384)$ and is untested.

\section{Algorithms}
\textbf{A. Carry-chain local-to-global certification.} Enumerate the $2\beta^2$ reachable triples, check a candidate step table on each, and conclude exact correctness at every depth; $O(\beta^2)$ for the certificate, $O(n)$ per depth-$n$ confirmation.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{B. Boundary-gain / preconditioner spectrum.} Form $P=\|e\|^2I+WW^\T$, evaluate $\langle g,Pg\rangle=\|e\|^2\|g\|^2+\|W^\T g\|^2$, compare with the bound $dc^2\|g\|^2$, and report $\lambda_{\max}(P)/\lambda_{\min}(P)$; $O(h^2d)$ to form $P$, $O(hd)$ per gain, $O(h^3)$ for the spectrum.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{C. Horizon estimation and log-depth prediction.} The horizon is the least $N$ with $\lambda^N\Delta R<\gamma$; the depth bought by gain $m$ is $\lceil\log m/\log(1/\lambda)\rceil$; composing with the $d$-linear gain gives $N(d)=N_0+\lceil\log(d/d_0)/\log(1/\lambda)\rceil$. $O(1)$.

\section{Predictions}
\textbf{P1 --- logarithmic depth law.} Sweeping $d\in\{20,28,40,64,96,160,256,384\}$ should give usable depths equally spaced on a $\log d$ axis with slope $1/\log(1/\lambda)$; a sharp threshold in $(28,384)$ would falsify the mechanism.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{P2 --- scale invariance (sharpest test).} The proved gain depends on $\|e\|^2$, not on $d$ as such. Initialising the wide EOS at per-coordinate scale $c/\sqrt d$, holding $\|e\|^2$ fixed, should make the cure disappear.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{P3 --- stability window.} The same preconditioner bounds the stable step size, roughly $\eta<1/(dc^2+\|W\|^2)$; wide-EOS arms should optimise at a learning rate smaller by a factor $\approx 20/384$ and diverge, not merely slow, above it.

\section{Scope}
Proved: exact length-generality and local-to-global transfer for the carry transition; expressivity invariance of the boundary pathway in the EOS width; the closed form of the induced drift, the exact and lower-bounded gains, sharpness at $W=0$, exponential contraction and the $1/d$ budget; the existence of a state horizon for every bounded linear readout and the logarithmic depth shift. Not proved, and explicitly flagged: that improved boundary conditioning is \emph{the} reason the trained digit readout stays in distribution at depth. Section~4 analyses the boundary pathway in isolation and Section~5 models the cell in its contractive regime; both idealisations are chosen so that the conclusions are conservative --- a lower bound on gain and an upper bound on usable depth. The empirical strength of the round rests on the identical-weights construction, which cannot be circular: exactly one architectural variable moves.

\section{Discussion}
Three intuitions are overturned. \emph{``Bigger is better''}: a raw model with $471{,}582$ parameters fails where one with $335{,}242$ succeeds, and the boundary pathway's function class is width-independent, so extra capacity in the wrong place buys nothing. \emph{``Better features are the cure''}: an untrained random projection cures the task, so the learned encoder was never load-bearing --- its incidental contribution was a wide input space and therefore a wide EOS vector. \emph{``Position information fixes length generalisation''}: adding a sinusoidal position code left accuracy at $0.0049$; the transition never needed positional help and the readout needs margin, not coordinates.

What replaces them is a statement about boundary conditions. The final step is where an accumulated, geometrically shrinking state must be converted into a fine-grained symbolic decision, and everything determining how well that conversion is learned flows through the input pathway at that one step. Widening it does not change what the model \emph{can} do; it changes the geometry of the optimisation that decides what the model \emph{will} do. The practical corollary is cheap: give the terminal decoding step a rich, well-scaled input pathway --- padding an otherwise raw input to a wide space costs $364$ dead dimensions and no information, and flipped four out of four seeds to perfect length-$8$ accuracy.

\section{Conclusion}
A recurrent machine that has provably learned an exactly length-general transition can still fail at depth, because a contractive state and a bounded readout have a finite horizon. What extends that horizon is not capacity, not learned feature content, and not positional information. It is the richness of the input pathway at the final step, invisible to the function class and visible to the optimiser, entering the guaranteed gradient-flow gain linearly in its width and the usable depth logarithmically. In the controlled comparison motivating this analysis, moving that single variable --- with byte-identical cell and head weights --- took length-$8$ full-sequence accuracy from $0.0259$ to $1.0000$.

